<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad of Ghor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">assassinated <a href="/years/1206">1206</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> was a ruler of the Ghurid dynasty who conquered much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the late twelfth century. His victory at the second Battle of Tarain in 1192 opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> to Muslim rule. After his assassination in 1206, his general Qutb al-Din Aibak founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor.jpg" alt="Muhammad of Ghor" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27izz_al-Din_Muhammad._AH_599-602_AD_1171-1206.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a>, also called Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad ibn Sam, was born in Ghur in what is now west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, the son of the Ghurid ruler Baha al-Din Sam I, who died in 1149. His birth year, about 1144, is worked out from the writings of Juzjani, who makes him three years and a few months younger than his brother Ghiyath al-Din, born in 1140. Their uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a> imprisoned both brothers, suspecting their loyalty, until his son freed them after his death in 1161. The brothers then ruled as a dyarchy. Ghiyath al-Din governed the west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a> and was engaged against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a>, while Muhammad extended Ghurid rule eastwards from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Muhammad crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> in 1175 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a> he was wounded and routed by a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs led by the Chaulukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mularaja">Mularaja</a>, so he changed his route. He expelled the Ghaznavids by 1186, taking the upper Indus plain and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>.</p><p>In 1191 he was wounded at the first Battle of Tarain, defeated by the Rajput Confederacy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>, and returned to Khurasan. A year later he came back with a large army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> and won the second battle at the same place, and he executed Prithviraj shortly afterwards. In 1194 he defeated the Gahadavala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a> at Chandawar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> was looted and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a> annexed in 1198. Most operations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were left to slave commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a>, who pushed the frontier as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>.</p><p>After Ghiyath al-Din died on 13 March 1203, Muhammad became supreme sultan. He suffered a heavy defeat at Andkhud against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a>, who were aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>, and lost most of Khurasan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>While Muhammad was planning to avenge Andkhud, a rebellion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khokhars">Khokhars</a> drew him to the Salt Range, where he crushed it. On 15 March 1206 he was assassinated at Dhamiak while at evening prayers. Sources name the Khokhars or Isma'ili emissaries. His only child, a daughter, had died before him, and his slaves and the Ghurid elite quarrelled over the succession. Muhammad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> annexed the Ghurid lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> by 1215, but the conquests east of the Indus became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> under Aibak. At their height the Ghurids ruled a territory 3,000 km across, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad of Ghor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Delhi Sultanate began when the Ghurid Empire broke up after the assassination of <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b> in 1206.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1186 Mu'izz al-Din <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b> took Lahore and imprisoned Khusrau Malik, the last Ghaznavid sultan, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…40,000 under Tayangu and the Karakhanid rulers Uthman ibn Ibrahim and Taj al-Din Bilge Khan relieved Khwarazm from <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b>, chased the Ghurids to Andkhud and besieged the sultan in its castle until Uthman negotiated his ransom; in 1205 the Ghurid viceroy of Balkh seized Tirmidh and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate’s army had begun as the Turkic slave soldiers of <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b>, its nucleus were household cavalry archers of slave origin, and the sultans bought Turks to build a corps of mounted archers whose loyalty was to their master, not to any people.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After Prithviraj Chauhan (Prithviraja III) of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari fell to <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b> in 1192, his son Govindaraja was installed as a Ghurid vassal and moved to the fortress of Ranthambore (Ranastambhapura) in eastern Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-ranastambhapura">Chahamanas of Ranastambhapura</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithviraja III defeated <b>Muhammad of Ghor</b> at the first battle of Tarain in 1191, but lost the second in 1192 and was executed.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ghor. Died in Dhamiak, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.12,73.28&amp;year=1206" aria-label="Dhamiak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6426 961 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Muhammad of Ghor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6876 1231h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6876 1231h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.12,73.28&amp;year=1206">What was here in 1206? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
